# apps/scrum-poker-client

The browser UI for scrum-poker.shatteredarchive.dev (Vite + React 19, dev port 63080, with
`/api`, `/health` and `/ws/scrum` proxied to scrum-poker-server on 63000). All domain types,
deck rules and the wire protocol come from `@shatteredarchive/scrum-poker-core`, so the client
and server share one definition of a legal deck and one message contract.

Design tenets:

- **The server owns room state; nothing is optimistic.** A card renders as selected because
  the server echoed it back, so what you see is what the room sees. Planning poker is
  low-frequency — the round trip is invisible, and the alternative briefly shows a card that
  lies.
- **Two routes, no router library.** `routing/useRoute.ts` is ~40 lines of `pushState` +
  `popstate`. Room URLs are real paths (`/room/<uuid>`) meant to be pasted into chat, which is
  why the client image's nginx conf serves index.html for unknown paths. The path pattern stays
  permissive rather than matching the UUID grammar, so pre-switch numeric room links still work.
- **Light and dark are equals.** `index.css` defines both as CSS-variable blocks keyed off a
  `data-theme` attribute that an inline script in index.html sets BEFORE first paint (no white
  flash); `theme/useTheme.ts` adopts that decision rather than recomputing it. `color-scheme`
  is set per theme so native controls follow.
- **One ad slot, off by default.** `features/shared/AdSlot.tsx` renders a single bounded block,
  and nothing at all — no container, no placeholder, and no request to any ad network — unless
  both `VITE_AD_CLIENT` and `VITE_AD_SLOT` were set at build time. (The loader code still sits
  in the bundle as dead code; it can't be tree-shaken because the injectable `config` prop that
  makes the component testable stops the minifier proving the branch unreachable. The guarantee
  is no ad REQUEST, not no ad code.) Those arrive via Vite `define`
  constants, not `import.meta.env`, because this repo's Jest client setup cannot compile
  `import.meta` anywhere in a module graph (see game-client's features/auth/authFragment.ts) —
  using it would make AdSlot and every importer untestable.

What `storage.ts` keeps per room is a `participantSecret`, not the participant id. The id is in
every roster the server broadcasts, so it identifies a row to render, never a person to trust;
the secret is what re-attaches you to your seat after a refresh. Treat it like the host token.

`net/useScrumRoom.ts` holds the socket and is where the subtle behaviour lives: it reconnects
with capped exponential backoff, re-joins automatically when it finds itself missing from the
roster (which is what makes "clear all users" drop ghosts while live participants reappear
untouched), and deliberately does NOT auto-rejoin after an idle eviction — it branches on the
server's error `code`, never on message text, because auto-rejoining there would defeat the
one-hour sweep it came from. That suppression also covers the RECONNECT path (`onopen` skips
the join while evicted): a dropped connection is not activity, so re-joining there would put an
idled-out person back on the roster without them touching anything.
